<h2>What UTM parameters do</h2><p>UTM parameters are query-string labels commonly used to identify where campaign traffic came from. A consistent taxonomy makes analytics reports easier to group, compare and audit across teams.</p><h2>How to use this UTM builder</h2><ol><li>Paste the final landing-page URL.</li><li>Set a source such as a platform, publication or partner.</li><li>Use medium for the channel classification, such as email, cpc or paid-social.</li><li>Name the campaign consistently across all related assets.</li><li>Use term and content only when they add useful segmentation.</li><li>Copy the generated URL into the campaign destination field.</li></ol><h2>Use a naming convention before campaign volume grows</h2><p>Decide rules for capitalization, spaces, dates and abbreviations before multiple people start tagging links. This builder can normalize values to lowercase hyphenated strings, helping prevent <em>LinkedIn</em>, <em>linkedin</em> and <em>linked_in</em> from appearing as separate labels.</p><h2>Do not put sensitive data in UTM values</h2><p>UTM values are part of a URL and may appear in browser history, analytics systems, logs and shared links. Keep them descriptive but non-sensitive.</p>
<?php $toolContent=ob_get_clean();$faqs=[['What is utm_source?','It identifies the traffic source or platform, such as google, linkedin or newsletter.'],['What is utm_medium?','It classifies the marketing channel, such as cpc, email or paid-social.'],['Should UTM values use spaces?','Consistency matters most. This tool can convert values to lowercase hyphenated strings to reduce naming variants.'],['Can I use UTM parameters for internal links?','Usually avoid using campaign UTMs on internal site links because they can overwrite or fragment attribution. Use analytics events or other internal tracking approaches instead.']];require __DIR__.'/../includes/tool-template.php';
